---
registryId: practice-ride-sharing
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is a ride-sharing system?

**A ride-sharing system coordinates a time-sensitive marketplace between riders and drivers.**
It receives fresh driver locations, finds eligible nearby supply, commits one
driver to one trip, guides both parties through a shared trip lifecycle, and settles the
fare after the ride.

The hard part is not drawing cars on a map. Location data is fast, approximate, and
short-lived; trip, payment, and safety decisions are durable business facts. The design
must use each kind of consistency deliberately.

The core invariant is: **one active accepted trip per rider and driver, with one durable owner for every accepted transition.** A stale map
can recover. Conflicting trip ownership cannot be exposed to users.

{% callout variant="info" %}
This walkthrough uses explicit interview assumptions rather than production claims. If
you need a foundation first, review [ACID transactions](/fundamentals/acid-properties),
[message queues](/fundamentals/message-queues), and the
[system design interview framework](/fundamentals/system-design-framework).
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% accordion defaultOpen="clarifying" %}

{% accordion-item id="clarifying" title="1. Clarify requirements and scope" %}

### Start with the marketplace contract

A useful first version supports one rider, one driver, and one direct trip inside a
single operating region. Ask which user-visible promises matter before choosing a geo
index, stream, or database.

{% concept-grid columns="2" %}
{% concept-card title="Core rider and driver actions" eyebrow="Functional" icon="route" tone="blue" %}
- A rider requests an immediate trip, sees an estimate, and cancels when policy allows.
- An available driver publishes location, receives offers, and accepts or declines.
- Both parties see assignment, arrival, pickup, in-trip progress, and completion.
- The system calculates the final fare, starts payment, and issues a receipt.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Service promises" eyebrow="Non-functional" icon="gauge" tone="green" %}
- Return an initial match outcome within 5 seconds at P95 in a healthy region.
- Keep active-trip state available during failures even if new matching is paused.
- Bound location age; never present an indefinitely stale driver as available.
- Make trip commands and payment attempts safe to retry.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Safety and privacy boundaries" eyebrow="Guardrails" icon="shield" tone="rose" %}
- Isolate urgent safety reports from ordinary notification and payment capacity.
- Restrict precise-location access by role, purpose, region, and retention policy.
- Preserve auditable trip and safety events without retaining every raw coordinate forever.
- Minimize exposed contact and payment data through tokenization and scoped access.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="First-version boundary" eyebrow="Out of scope" icon="boxes" tone="neutral" %}
- No scheduled rides, shared rides, or multi-stop route optimization.
- No driver repositioning model or long-term demand forecasting.
- No globally roaming trip that changes its authoritative region mid-ride.
- No in-house card network, maps engine, or emergency-response organization.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Resolve the questions that change the architecture

1. **How many drivers are concurrently online?** Daily active drivers do not all send
   location at once, so concurrency determines stream load.
2. **How fresh must an offer be?** A four-second update interval does not imply every
   indexed point is four seconds old; ingestion delay, disconnects, and retries add age.
3. **Who owns a trip?** Choose one authoritative operating region from the pickup area
   and keep ownership stable through completion.
4. **How are offers sent?** Sequential offers reduce contention but can increase match
   time; small bounded batches improve latency but create acceptance races.
5. **What may degrade?** New estimates and matches may pause. Active-trip recovery and
   the safety path require stricter continuity.

{% callout variant="tip" %}
State the distinction explicitly: location is a hint used to choose candidates; an
atomic trip claim is the decision that creates ownership.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="calculations" title="2. Estimate geospatial and dispatch scale" %}

### Use concurrency and peaks, not only daily totals

Assume **10 million trips per day**, **2 million concurrently online drivers** at peak,
a **four-second location interval**, and a **10x peak-to-average trip multiplier**. The
numbers are a model to stress, not a claim about a specific company.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="500K/s" label="Peak location updates" detail="2M online drivers / 4 seconds" tone="cyan" /%}
{% metric value="60 MB/s" label="Raw location ingress" detail="500K updates/s x 120 modeled bytes" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="116/s" label="Average trip creation" detail="10M trips / 86,400 seconds" tone="green" /%}
{% metric value="~1.2K/s" label="Planned peak matches" detail="Average rounded up with a 10x peak factor" tone="violet" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Expose the coupled capacity decisions

Location cadence controls write pressure. Search expansion and local supply density
control candidate pressure. Change both sides of the model and identify which stage
crosses its headroom target first.

{% interactive-block
   id="practice/ride-sharing-dispatch-capacity-lab"
   dataFile="/api/content/practice/ride-sharing/data/dispatch-capacity-model.json" /%}

### Translate arithmetic into architecture

- **Location stream:** `online drivers / update interval` gives ingress events per
  second. At the modeled peak, one sustained hour is about 216 GB of raw payload before
  replication, indexes, and protocol overhead.
- **Geo-index memory:** size the concurrently available set and object overhead, not all
  historical driver accounts. Benchmark the actual representation and account for skew.
- **Candidate work:** one hexagonal `k`-ring contains `1 + 3k(k + 1)` cells before edge
  cases. Stop expanding once enough fresh, eligible candidates exist.
- **History retention:** a 24-hour worst-case upper bound at sustained peak is about
  5.18 TB raw. Real sizing needs the traffic duty cycle, compression, sampling, legal
  requirements, and replica count.
- **Network fan-out:** rider and driver live updates are much smaller than broadcasting
  every driver to every client. Publish only trip-scoped or viewport-scoped changes.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Do not divide total registered or daily active drivers by the update interval. Only
concurrently online sessions produce the peak location stream.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="architecture" title="3. Draw the high-level architecture" %}

### Sketch the two critical paths first

The location path continuously refreshes an expendable regional view. The dispatch path
reads that view, computes candidates, and then commits one durable assignment. Keeping
these paths separate prevents telemetry volume from turning every trip transition into a
high-write transaction.

{% callout variant="info" %}
Use the whiteboard before reading the reference. Show regional ownership, the hot
location index, the durable trip store, an event outbox, and a safety path that does not
depend on payment or ordinary notifications.
{% /callout %}

{% design-challenge
   challengeId="ride-sharing"
   title="Design the regional dispatch and trip path"
   prompt="Two million drivers may be online while riders need a match outcome within five seconds. Show sequenced location ingestion, bounded geo search, ETA scoring, one atomic driver claim, durable trip events, payment recovery, and an isolated safety workflow." /%}

{% system-flow title="Hot location path" caption="The index is a regional, replaceable projection. Sequence and freshness prevent old packets and disconnected drivers from becoming candidates." %}
{% system-node title="Driver app" eyebrow="Publish" icon="map-pin" tone="cyan" %}
Send driver ID, coordinates, event time, monotonic sequence, accuracy, heading, and
availability lease renewal.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Regional ingest" eyebrow="Validate" icon="radio" tone="blue" %}
Authenticate, rate-limit, reject impossible or old updates, and partition the stream by
driver ID.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Location projector" eyebrow="Order" icon="activity" tone="violet" %}
Consume one driver's updates in order, reject lower sequences, and move the driver
between geo cells.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Hot geo index" eyebrow="Serve" icon="search" tone="green" %}
Return nearby available drivers with update age and vehicle attributes; expire abandoned
leases automatically.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

{% system-flow title="Ride request and durable assignment" caption="Candidate discovery may be eventually consistent. Driver ownership changes only through one authoritative conditional write." %}
{% system-node title="Rider API" eyebrow="Request" icon="smartphone" tone="blue" %}
Validate pickup, destination, product, rider eligibility, idempotency key, and regional
ownership.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Dispatch service" eyebrow="Choose" icon="route" tone="amber" %}
Expand nearby cells, filter stale or ineligible drivers, estimate pickup time, rank, and
send a bounded offer batch.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Trip service" eyebrow="Commit" icon="shield-check" tone="violet" %}
Atomically claim rider and driver, advance the trip version, and persist an outbox event.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Async workflows" eyebrow="React" icon="git-branch" tone="green" %}
Notify clients, update analytics, start payment work, and feed support systems without
holding the assignment transaction open.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Define ownership boundaries

- **Regional edge and session gateways** own authentication, admission control, and
  client connections.
- **Location service** owns recent position, sequence, freshness, and geo-cell membership.
- **Dispatch service** owns candidate policy and offer orchestration, but not final trip
  ownership.
- **Trip service** owns the authoritative lifecycle, active rider/driver claims, fare
  inputs, and outbox.
- **Payment service** owns provider attempts, internal ledger references, reconciliation,
  and receipts.
- **Safety service** owns durable urgent reports, escalation state, access audit, and
  responder handoff.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="data" title="4. Model trip state, storage, and contracts" %}

### Separate authoritative facts from projections

One datastore does not need to serve every access pattern. Choose storage around
ownership, update rate, query shape, retention, and recovery.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Authoritative relational state" eyebrow="Trips and money" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
Store trips, active-party claims, state versions, fare inputs, payment references,
idempotency records, and an outbox. Transactions protect cross-row invariants.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Regional hot projections" eyebrow="Location and sessions" icon="map-pin" tone="cyan" %}
Store current driver position, cell, availability lease, connection route, and update
sequence. Rebuild these projections from sessions or a retained stream after failure.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Append-oriented history" eyebrow="Events and analytics" icon="activity" tone="violet" %}
Retain trip events, selected location samples, pricing decisions, and audit records in
streams and analytical storage with purpose-specific retention.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Make the trip state machine monotonic

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Request and search" label="REQUESTED -> SEARCHING" %}
Reserve the rider's active-trip slot, store pickup and product constraints, and start a
bounded matching deadline.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Assign and approach" label="DRIVER_ASSIGNED -> ARRIVING" %}
Commit one driver claim, record the accepted offer, and publish assignment after the
transaction commits.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Start and travel" label="IN_PROGRESS" %}
Require an authorized actor and expected state version. Begin fare inputs and trip-scoped
tracking without allowing a late cancellation to rewrite history.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Complete and settle" label="COMPLETED" %}
Commit immutable completion inputs, then let payment advance through its own pending,
captured, failed, or reconciled states.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

Cancellation is a terminal transition with a reason, actor, timestamp, and policy
version. It does not delete the prior lifecycle. Payment state must never force a
completed trip back to `IN_PROGRESS`.

### Enforce one active assignment

The following executable model demonstrates three ideas: condition the claim on an
expected driver version, store a command result for retry, and emit one outbox event with
the committed state. A production implementation would perform the state changes and
idempotency write in one database transaction.

{% code-block
   file="/api/content/practice/ride-sharing/code/atomic-driver-claim.js"
   language="javascript"
   title="Retry-safe atomic driver claim model" /%}

### Design records around access patterns

- `trip`: immutable ID, owner region, rider, driver, product, state, state version,
  pickup, destination, fare inputs, and lifecycle timestamps.
- `active_party_claim`: party type plus party ID as a unique key, trip ID, lease/state,
  and version. Release only through a valid terminal transition.
- `trip_command`: idempotency key, actor, expected version, request hash, result, and
  expiry policy.
- `trip_event_outbox`: event ID, trip ID as ordering key, event type, state version,
  payload version, and publish status.
- `driver_location_hot`: driver ID, cell ID, coordinates, accuracy, event time, sequence,
  and lease expiry.
- `payment_attempt`: trip-scoped idempotency key, provider reference, amount, currency,
  status, and reconciliation timestamps.

Partition streams by the entity whose order matters. Driver ID preserves one driver's
location sequence; trip ID preserves one trip's lifecycle sequence. Apache Kafka's
[official documentation](https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/) describes ordering as
a per-partition guarantee, not a global topic order.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="deep-dive" title="5. Deep-dive matching, location, payments, and safety" %}

### Match in stages so expensive work stays bounded

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Find cells" label="Geo filter" %}
Map pickup coordinates to a cell and search the pickup cell first. Expand neighboring
rings only until the candidate budget or deadline is met.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Reject impossible candidates" label="Eligibility" %}
Remove stale leases, wrong products, accessibility mismatches, active trips, policy
blocks, and drivers already holding an offer that cannot overlap.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Estimate pickup time" label="ETA" %}
Use road-network travel time for a small candidate set. Straight-line distance is a cheap
prefilter, not the final pickup estimate.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Offer and claim" label="Commit" %}
Send one or a small bounded batch of expiring offers. The first valid conditional claim
wins; all later accepts receive the committed result.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

The score can combine pickup ETA, destination compatibility, driver idle time, product
rules, and marketplace objectives. Log feature values, policy version, candidate set,
and rejection reasons so teams can diagnose unfair or unstable outcomes. Do not treat a
complex optimizer as a substitute for the atomic claim.

### Treat the location stream as a lossy sensor feed

- **Order per driver:** attach a monotonic session sequence; reject lower sequences even
  when network retries arrive later.
- **Freshness:** store event time, receive time, and lease expiry. Candidate filtering
  should use a measured age bound rather than assuming the nominal update interval.
- **Backpressure:** coalesce queued updates for the same driver because the newest valid
  position supersedes intermediate points for matching.
- **Hot-cell skew:** partition with a stable region/cell strategy, isolate event venues,
  and cap query work before a dense cell dominates one shard.
- **Recovery:** rebuild hot indexes from active sessions or a short retained stream; do
  not make raw long-term location history the only recovery source.

Hierarchical cells are an indexing tool, not an exact distance answer. The
[H3 indexing guide](https://h3geo.org/docs/highlights/indexing/) explains its hierarchy
and approximate geometric containment, while the
[Redis GEOSEARCH reference](https://redis.io/docs/latest/commands/geosearch/) documents
radius and box queries plus their candidate-sensitive complexity. Benchmark the chosen
implementation with city density and boundary cases.

### Keep pricing, payment, and safety off the assignment transaction

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Pricing" %}
Compute a quote from product, estimated distance/time, fees, and a versioned regional
marketplace policy. Store the quote inputs and policy version. Smooth zone-level supply
and demand signals, cap changes by policy, and avoid oscillating the rider price on every
location event.
{% /tab %}
{% tab title="Payment" %}
Tokenize payment credentials with a provider. Optionally authorize before pickup, then
commit final fare inputs at completion and capture asynchronously with a stable
trip-scoped idempotency key. A timeout creates an uncertain payment state to reconcile;
it does not reverse trip completion. Stripe's official documentation covers
[idempotent requests](https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests) and
[separate authorization and capture](https://docs.stripe.com/payments/place-a-hold-on-a-payment-method).
{% /tab %}
{% tab title="Safety" %}
Give urgent reports a dedicated authenticated endpoint, durable acknowledgement,
high-priority workflow, restricted access, immutable audit trail, and accountable human
handoff. Keep this path independent of matching, ordinary notifications, pricing, and
payment availability.
{% /tab %}
{% /tabs %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="reliability" title="6. Scale regions and design recovery" %}

### Make a region the unit of dispatch ownership

Route a pickup to one operating region and keep its trip authority there. Within the
region, partition hot location and dispatch work by cells or stable cell groups, then
replicate durable trip state across failure domains. Global services can distribute
configuration and identity, but a normal match should not require global consensus.

### Explore failures as state transitions

Choose a failure and then select a recovery contract. The result shows whether the
system preserves one trip owner, bounded location freshness, one intended charge, or an
independent safety response.

{% interactive-block
   id="practice/ride-sharing-trip-recovery-lab"
   dataFile="/api/content/practice/ride-sharing/data/trip-recovery-scenarios.json" /%}

### Define degraded modes before the incident

- **Hot geo index unavailable**
  - Stop new offers in affected cells or use a strictly bounded last-known projection.
  - Preserve active-trip state and client reconnect; do not assign from an unbounded
    stale snapshot.
- **Matcher overload**
  - Admit by region and product, reduce candidate caps, stop ring expansion sooner, and
    return an honest delayed/no-supply result.
  - Do not let retries multiply work without a request deadline and idempotency key.
- **Trip database failover**
  - Fence the old writer before promoting another authority.
  - Pause new assignments when ownership is uncertain; continue reads and safety intake
    from known durable state.
- **Event stream lag**
  - Keep trip commits available through a transactional outbox.
  - Let notifications and analytics catch up from durable state; alert on oldest event
    age and state-version gaps.
- **Payment provider outage**
  - Complete trips, mark payment pending, retry with one identity, and reconcile later.
  - Keep limits and risk policy for riders with unresolved balances.
- **Safety dependency outage**
  - Fall back to an independently operated escalation route with durable local evidence.
  - Page on acknowledgement and responder deadlines, not only HTTP availability.

### Measure user promises and invariants

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Dispatch health" icon="gauge" tone="blue" %}
Track request-to-offer and request-to-assignment latency, no-candidate rate, offer
acceptance, candidate count, search rings, stale-candidate rejection, and claim conflicts.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Trip correctness" icon="shield-check" tone="green" %}
Track invalid transition attempts, duplicate command suppression, active-party claim
conflicts, outbox lag, client state-version gaps, and reconciliation repairs.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Operational safety" icon="activity" tone="rose" %}
Track location age, hot-cell skew, regional saturation, payment-pending age, safety-event
acknowledgement, responder pickup, and recovery drill outcomes.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Run drills for duplicate accepts, stale driver leases, a fenced regional failover,
notification lag, an uncertain payment capture, and an unavailable ordinary queue during
a safety report. A dashboard cannot prove recovery; a drill can.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="tradeoffs" title="7. Compare trade-offs and alternatives" %}

### Defend decisions against the stated workload

{% concept-grid columns="2" %}
{% concept-card title="Cell index vs spatial tree" eyebrow="Candidate discovery" icon="map" tone="cyan" %}
Hierarchical cells make bucketing, neighbors, and regional partitioning explicit, but
need boundary expansion and exact distance/ETA checks. Spatial trees adapt to density but
add rebalancing and ownership complexity. Choose from measured query and update patterns.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Sequential vs batch offers" eyebrow="Matching" icon="users" tone="violet" %}
Sequential offers reduce races and rejected accepts but add round-trip delay. Small
parallel batches improve response time but require expiring offers and one atomic winner.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Ephemeral index vs durable location log" eyebrow="Telemetry" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
An ephemeral hot index is cheap to overwrite and rebuild. A retained stream supports
replay and audit. Keeping every raw point forever increases privacy, storage, and access
risk without improving current matching.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Regional authority vs global writer" eyebrow="Ownership" icon="globe" tone="amber" %}
Regional authority lowers matching latency and limits failures, but needs explicit trip
handoff policy. A global writer simplifies uniqueness conceptually while adding a broad
latency and availability dependency.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Strong trip state vs eventual projections" eyebrow="Consistency" icon="shield" tone="green" %}
Use conditional, durable writes for assignment and lifecycle. Let maps, ETAs,
notifications, and analytics converge from versioned events because brief display lag is
repairable.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Availability vs bounded refusal" eyebrow="Failure" icon="circle-alert" tone="rose" %}
Rejecting a new ride during uncertain ownership protects users from conflicting drivers.
Active trips and safety events deserve higher continuity than new matching or fresh
pricing.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Alternatives rejected for this target

- **One transactional write for every GPS packet:** ties telemetry throughput to the
  authoritative trip database and pays strong-consistency cost for replaceable hints.
- **Broadcast every nearby driver to the rider:** leaks precise supply data, increases
  client fan-out, and creates a moving consistency problem. Send only the matched driver
  and product-level estimates.
- **Run global optimal matching on every request:** may improve a theoretical objective,
  but exceeds a five-second interaction budget under large, changing candidate sets.
  Use bounded regional optimization and measure marketplace outcomes.
- **Let last-write-wins resolve assignment:** hides races by overwriting a promise. Use a
  conditional claim that returns one winner and a visible conflict.
- **Make payment success the terminal trip state:** couples mobility truth to a separate
  financial workflow and makes provider outages corrupt the lifecycle.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="wrap-up" title="8. Wrap up, risks, and extensions" %}

### Recommended design in one minute

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Own by pickup region" label="Route" %}
Choose one low-latency authority for the trip and avoid global coordination on the normal
dispatch path.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Project fresh supply" label="Locate" %}
Partition sequenced driver updates, reject old packets, and maintain a lease-bound hot
geo index.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Bound candidate work" label="Match" %}
Expand cells progressively, filter cheaply, calculate ETA for a capped set, and send
expiring offers.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Commit one owner" label="Claim" %}
Use a versioned conditional transaction for rider, driver, trip, idempotency result, and
outbox event.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="5" title="Separate side effects" label="Complete" %}
Advance trip state monotonically while notifications, payment, analytics, and support
consume versioned events.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="6" title="Degrade by priority" label="Recover" %}
Pause uncertain new matches, preserve active trips, reconcile payments, and isolate the
safety workflow.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Highest risks

1. **A hot event cell overloads one geo shard.** Track per-cell update/query skew, split
   ownership before saturation, and cap candidate scans.
2. **Two accepts create conflicting assignments.** Prove the conditional claim under
   concurrency and make conflict rate observable.
3. **Stale or reordered GPS creates bad offers.** Use sequence, event age, lease expiry,
   and stale-candidate rejection metrics.
4. **A regional failover creates two writers.** Fence the former authority before
   accepting new trip transitions.
5. **Payment retries create duplicate charges.** Reuse one operation identity and
   reconcile uncertain provider results against the internal ledger.
6. **A safety report shares a failed dependency.** Test independent admission, durable
   acknowledgement, escalation, and restricted audit access.

### Extensions worth rehearsing

- **Scheduled rides**
  - Add reservation windows, driver commitment policy, and a transition from planned to
    active regional ownership.
  - Recalculate supply promises because scheduled demand competes with immediate rides.
- **Shared rides and multiple stops**
  - Replace one rider-one trip assumptions with seat capacity, route insertion, changing
    ETAs, split fares, and per-passenger cancellation policy.
- **Airport and event queues**
  - Model geofenced pickup zones, first-in-first-out eligibility, venue-specific staging,
    and burst admission independent of ordinary nearest-driver matching.
- **Cross-region trips**
  - Keep one trip authority or execute an explicit fenced handoff; never let both regions
    advance the lifecycle concurrently.
- **Marketplace optimization**
  - Add measured objectives for pickup time, cancellations, driver idle time, earnings,
    accessibility, and pricing stability.
  - Version policies and evaluate outcomes by city, time, product, and protected slices.

### Primary implementation references

- [H3 indexing and hierarchy](https://h3geo.org/docs/highlights/indexing/)
- [Redis GEOSEARCH command](https://redis.io/docs/latest/commands/geosearch/)
- [Apache Kafka event ordering and partitions](https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/)
- [PostgreSQL row-level locking](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html)
- [Stripe idempotent requests](https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests)
- [Stripe authorization and capture](https://docs.stripe.com/payments/place-a-hold-on-a-payment-method)

{% callout variant="tip" %}
End the interview by tracing one driver update, one successful assignment, one duplicate
accept, and one payment timeout. Name the owner, invariant, and recovery metric at every
step.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

